1
00:00:09,707 --> 00:00:19,550
[SPEAKER_02]: There's these four types of, commonly translated as, grasping, attachment, or assumptions, as you like to say.

2
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:20,350
[SPEAKER_02]: Upādānas.

3
00:00:20,610 --> 00:00:21,730
[SPEAKER_02]: Four types of upādānas.

4
00:00:22,291 --> 00:00:24,691
[SPEAKER_02]: The one I'm interested in is dīttupādāna.

5
00:00:25,551 --> 00:00:27,052
[SPEAKER_02]: So, assumption to views.

6
00:00:27,772 --> 00:00:27,972
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

7
00:00:27,992 --> 00:00:30,333
[SPEAKER_02]: Commonly translated as grasping to views.

8
00:00:30,753 --> 00:00:30,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

9
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:37,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, probably grasping is kind of... it implies that you're aware of what you're grasping towards.

10
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:47,004
[SPEAKER_01]: But you going towards things, it's already on the basis of false assumption or assumption in regard to those things.

11
00:00:47,064 --> 00:00:48,524
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you're already reaching out.

12
00:00:48,824 --> 00:00:50,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you assume that you can reach out.

13
00:00:54,187 --> 00:01:06,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when it comes to views, assumption to views, dhītu upādāna means basically that you're already not even aware that you're acting out of your views.

14
00:01:07,821 --> 00:01:17,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you need to undo your lack of awareness to become aware that you've been assuming the standpoint of a view.

15
00:01:20,788 --> 00:01:25,951
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, oh, I must stop holding fixed views as if it is a matter of choice.

16
00:01:26,791 --> 00:01:30,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Because as the Buddha said in that sutta, well, that view is fixed then.

17
00:01:30,788 --> 00:01:39,015
[SPEAKER_01]: When that man said, oh, my view is that no view is pleasing to me, which is what usually people chant in the temples and say, don't hold to fixed views.

18
00:01:40,457 --> 00:01:43,840
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha said, well, not hold... no view is pleasing to me?

19
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:45,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's surely pleasing to you.

20
00:01:46,642 --> 00:01:48,364
[SPEAKER_01]: And then this man said, oh, it is, isn't it?

21
00:01:49,224 --> 00:01:52,467
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I don't hold to fixed views is your fixed position.

22
00:01:53,428 --> 00:01:55,450
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just decide to let go of the views.

23
00:01:56,010 --> 00:01:56,591
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean.

24
00:01:57,716 --> 00:02:01,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Upādāna is not like grasping, because grasping, yeah, I'm not gonna grasp for this.

25
00:02:01,878 --> 00:02:04,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And people think, okay, so that's how I'm undoing my upādāna.

26
00:02:04,259 --> 00:02:04,439
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

27
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:06,620
[SPEAKER_01]: You undo your upādāna where you see it.

28
00:02:07,421 --> 00:02:10,843
[SPEAKER_01]: See, you can only take something at face value, until you know the real value.

29
00:02:12,663 --> 00:02:19,647
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only take something at the... for granted, a nature of something for granted, until you have understood that nature.

30
00:02:21,028 --> 00:02:26,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, even if it were the same as to what you assumed, you won't be assuming it, because you know it.

31
00:02:26,431 --> 00:02:29,793
[SPEAKER_01]: So, assumption and knowledge are mutually exclusive.

32
00:02:30,894 --> 00:02:33,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of knowledge means presence of assumption.

33
00:02:34,296 --> 00:02:36,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of knowledge means absence of assumption.

34
00:02:38,538 --> 00:02:39,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Dark and light.

35
00:02:40,339 --> 00:02:41,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence all the similes that we would give.

36
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:44,401
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's literally what assumption means, even in Greek.

37
00:02:44,682 --> 00:02:45,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Asumere.

38
00:02:45,482 --> 00:02:46,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Take for granted.

39
00:02:46,343 --> 00:02:48,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Take at the face value without knowing what that thing is.

40
00:02:50,745 --> 00:03:02,068
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, oh, so your whole, actually, existence, I am, and this is mine, these things are for me, that's already on the basis of that assumption, that these things are like that.

41
00:03:03,548 --> 00:03:06,809
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you wonder whether you assume things, well, ask yourself.

42
00:03:07,309 --> 00:03:09,790
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha said the one who is free from assumption is free from suffering.

43
00:03:10,930 --> 00:03:11,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I free from suffering?

44
00:03:11,930 --> 00:03:13,491
[SPEAKER_01]: No, means the assumptions are there.

45
00:03:14,231 --> 00:03:17,892
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't find them as the objects of your attention, because your attention

46
00:03:18,338 --> 00:03:20,859
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already rooted in that assumed basis.

47
00:03:21,539 --> 00:03:22,579
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the views are.

48
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:25,380
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the view is the most blameworthy thing, as the Buddha said.

49
00:03:25,641 --> 00:03:26,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Wrong view.

50
00:03:29,102 --> 00:03:35,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Because wrong view, everything is, well, going to be tainted by that wrongness of that view.

51
00:03:35,824 --> 00:03:37,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're doing right things.

52
00:03:41,606 --> 00:03:45,147
[SPEAKER_01]: So, an example of... Well, an example of wrong view, well, many.

53
00:03:46,188 --> 00:03:47,268
[SPEAKER_01]: A view of sensuality,

54
00:03:47,910 --> 00:03:48,811
[SPEAKER_01]: View of sense of self.

55
00:03:49,531 --> 00:03:50,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Atavāda, obviously.

56
00:03:50,732 --> 00:03:50,912
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

57
00:03:51,172 --> 00:03:51,792
[SPEAKER_02]: What is this for?

58
00:03:51,812 --> 00:03:57,656
[SPEAKER_02]: This is assumption of... to sensuality, assumption to views, assumption to... Virtue and duty.

59
00:03:58,496 --> 00:04:00,858
[SPEAKER_02]: And assumption to self.

60
00:04:01,198 --> 00:04:01,718
[SPEAKER_02]: Atavāda.

61
00:04:01,778 --> 00:04:02,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Atavāda.

62
00:04:02,219 --> 00:04:02,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right, right.

63
00:04:02,859 --> 00:04:03,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

64
00:04:03,199 --> 00:04:04,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

65
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:05,380
[SPEAKER_01]: They all share the same nature.

66
00:04:06,481 --> 00:04:07,562
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a different extent.

67
00:04:08,662 --> 00:04:13,385
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the same principle of you acting out of a standpoint that's based on ignorance.

68
00:04:14,526 --> 00:04:16,367
[SPEAKER_01]: It's based on your active ignoring of it.

69
00:04:18,525 --> 00:04:27,974
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it takes that effort to stop ignoring it and undo... undo, basically, your own lack of... lack of knowledge in regard to it.

70
00:04:29,015 --> 00:04:31,337
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how the assumption falls off.

71
00:04:31,477 --> 00:04:33,158
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you stop fueling the assumption.

72
00:04:34,279 --> 00:04:43,368
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just make a choice to not assume, because your choice is already a result of your assumption, of your view of what your existence is.

73
00:04:45,372 --> 00:04:49,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Of your view of what you are, what your sense of self is, what the ownership is.

74
00:04:50,275 --> 00:04:56,298
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we, like, encourage people to, like, question yourself, because that's how you start questioning what you took for granted.

75
00:04:57,179 --> 00:05:05,783
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember in my own case, like, I was doing walk meditation and sort of contemplating consciousness and five aggregates, and then it dawned on me.

76
00:05:06,364 --> 00:05:08,345
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm using all these terms and designations.

77
00:05:08,545 --> 00:05:14,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I say, oh, consciousness and it's like this and so that, but without what it is,

78
00:05:15,485 --> 00:05:15,945
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

79
00:05:16,286 --> 00:05:17,446
[SPEAKER_01]: That consciousness in itself.

80
00:05:17,607 --> 00:05:18,547
[SPEAKER_01]: The word that I'm using.

81
00:05:18,567 --> 00:05:20,028
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm clearly designating something.

82
00:05:20,188 --> 00:05:21,129
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I referring to?

83
00:05:21,709 --> 00:05:27,934
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I realized that even terminology I was using for myself, I didn't have clarity in regard to it.

84
00:05:29,075 --> 00:05:39,142
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can then that unclear terminology result in clarity of wisdom of that which precedes the terminology that you're trying to designate with those very terms?

85
00:05:40,507 --> 00:05:52,269
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, instead of trying to figure out what consciousness is and what's most pleasing sort of set up for what I think consciousness is, I thought, well, wait a minute, why don't you just say consciousness, consciousness?

86
00:05:53,085 --> 00:06:01,531
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, at least approximately, what am I referring to, right here, right now, in my experience, while I'm doing this walk meditation, when I say consciousness.

87
00:06:01,931 --> 00:06:11,797
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they realize, you people, and including myself at the time, using all like matter, form, consciousness, feeling, intentions, and then you build your practice on top of it.

88
00:06:11,938 --> 00:06:18,382
[SPEAKER_01]: But, wait a minute, what does those terms, at the very basic level, at the face value, what do they mean?

89
00:06:18,422 --> 00:06:19,663
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, I have no idea.

90
00:06:20,203 --> 00:06:29,348
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a vague notion of the meaning and I ignore that it's vague and then I carry on building up a pleasing structure that will result in magical product of enlightenment.

91
00:06:30,248 --> 00:06:33,810
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is... it's... that's why in the video I kept talking about undoing the wrong views.

92
00:06:34,971 --> 00:06:39,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like overdoing or doing on top of them until you climb onto the right view.

93
00:06:39,453 --> 00:06:40,034
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

94
00:06:40,074 --> 00:06:42,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Right view is by abandoning the wrong views.

95
00:06:43,015 --> 00:06:48,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Undoing the doing of wrong views, it's another way of saying abandon the wrong views.

96
00:06:50,690 --> 00:06:54,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Not abandoned by outrunning them, but abandoned by withdrawing yourself from the wrongness of them.

97
00:06:57,137 --> 00:06:57,958
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

98
00:06:58,018 --> 00:07:11,153
[SPEAKER_01]: You take all this... like I was taking feeling, like feeling-feeling, whatever it is, I was taking for granted that whatever ambiguous vague knowledge of feeling I had, I took it for granted that that's what feeling is.

99
00:07:12,527 --> 00:07:13,608
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's where the problem is.

100
00:07:13,628 --> 00:07:24,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because I don't find out what the feeling is, but because any movement in regard to feeling that I can do, it's done on the basis of me having taken it for granted, already implied that that's what the feeling is.

101
00:07:25,258 --> 00:07:26,960
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not what the feeling is.

102
00:07:26,980 --> 00:07:27,580
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I know that?

103
00:07:27,641 --> 00:07:29,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because I'm not free from suffering.

104
00:07:30,303 --> 00:07:39,391
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, if you were to truly understand the nature of feeling or any aggregate, you will be free from any taking for granted, any assumption, any craving, any suffering.

105
00:07:41,808 --> 00:07:42,928
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

106
00:07:42,988 --> 00:07:43,869
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about undoing.

107
00:07:44,389 --> 00:07:45,089
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a view.

108
00:07:47,109 --> 00:07:54,011
[SPEAKER_01]: I, gratuitously, was unaware that I was holding a view that I knew what the aggregates are, when I was thinking about them.

109
00:07:55,012 --> 00:07:59,013
[SPEAKER_01]: And on top of that view, then, I was trying to practice the dhamma of understanding what the aggregates were.

110
00:07:59,913 --> 00:08:03,814
[SPEAKER_01]: But once I realized that it's the opposite direction from where you were going with it.

111
00:08:03,934 --> 00:08:04,314
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no.

112
00:08:05,014 --> 00:08:07,315
[SPEAKER_01]: You practice the dhamma, but by undoing.

113
00:08:08,774 --> 00:08:09,895
[SPEAKER_01]: What you think these views are.

114
00:08:09,915 --> 00:08:12,216
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why everybody starts with the state of pūtūjana.

115
00:08:14,036 --> 00:08:20,840
[SPEAKER_01]: And the way to stop being a pūtūjana is to undo the aspects of your experience that are maintaining you as a pūtūjana.

116
00:08:21,440 --> 00:08:26,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to kind of, as I said, climb on top and reach some pinnacle whereby you suddenly become a sotāpanna.

117
00:08:27,323 --> 00:08:30,424
[SPEAKER_01]: You become a sotāpanna by undoing the doing of pūtūjana.

118
00:08:32,197 --> 00:08:40,483
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's almost, as you're saying, we assume and we begin in this place of purity, then we go about our lives and then... Yeah, exactly.

119
00:08:40,503 --> 00:08:47,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you come across the Buddhist teaching, it makes sense to a degree, and you don't question that, but then you just try to build on top of it.

120
00:08:47,788 --> 00:08:51,630
[SPEAKER_01]: But instead of building on top of it, you start questioning back to yourself.

121
00:08:52,191 --> 00:08:52,631
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait a minute.

122
00:08:53,031 --> 00:08:58,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I have no doubt, I'm not doubting, I'm not denying, that this sutta makes sense.

123
00:09:00,010 --> 00:09:10,396
[SPEAKER_01]: But, instead of now taking for granted that that's what the Buddha also meant by that sutta, let me then change or rather revise and see how much it makes sense.

124
00:09:10,496 --> 00:09:15,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, by the end of this sutta, people who understood it became arahants or at least sotāpānas.

125
00:09:16,420 --> 00:09:18,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I an arahant or at least a sotāpāna?

126
00:09:18,281 --> 00:09:18,441
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

127
00:09:18,861 --> 00:09:22,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means the degree of that sutta making sense to me is not sufficient.

128
00:09:22,903 --> 00:09:26,886
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of building on top of that degree in a direction of evolving the sense,

129
00:09:27,671 --> 00:09:32,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me undo what I think the Sutta is more and more, until I get to know what the Sutta is.

130
00:09:34,913 --> 00:09:36,213
[SPEAKER_01]: And you undo it by the way I said it.

131
00:09:36,453 --> 00:09:38,794
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Sutta talks about the four great elements.

132
00:09:38,934 --> 00:09:40,855
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a thought and concept of four great elements.

133
00:09:41,415 --> 00:09:48,256
[SPEAKER_01]: If that were truly what four great elements were, are, I would be free from suffering, or at least I would be a Sotāpanna.

134
00:09:49,337 --> 00:09:53,858
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when I think about the four great elements, that's not what the four great elements are, or rather,

135
00:09:54,448 --> 00:09:58,589
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm thinking about the four great elements affected with ignorance, assumption, craving and everything else.

136
00:09:59,050 --> 00:10:03,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why I think I can think about the four great elements, but at the same time not be an arahant.

137
00:10:05,492 --> 00:10:10,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Or I can think about the five aggregates objectively, without upādāna, but I'm not an arahant.

138
00:10:10,974 --> 00:10:12,214
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a contradiction in terms.

139
00:10:14,051 --> 00:10:17,312
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can think about it objectively, you'll be free from suffering.

140
00:10:17,812 --> 00:10:19,093
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know you're not free from suffering.

141
00:10:19,153 --> 00:10:24,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you must... you mustn't keep taking for granted what you think objective thinking is.

142
00:10:27,236 --> 00:10:35,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you have a measure of suffering that should be reducing the more... And when I say objective, I mean more aligned with the nature of how the aggregates actually are.

143
00:10:36,619 --> 00:10:40,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Impermanent, unsatisfactory, cannot be a base of ownership.

144
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:49,765
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's when the suttas, when monks won't understand something, you basically say it out loud, like, feeling, feeling.

145
00:10:50,106 --> 00:10:51,747
[SPEAKER_01]: What is feeling?

146
00:10:51,767 --> 00:10:56,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not like, let me think about and develop a concept of feeling, but feeling, feeling.

147
00:10:56,391 --> 00:10:59,133
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you designate it twice, presently.

148
00:10:59,773 --> 00:11:02,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, right here, right now, feeling, I'm feeling, I'm feeling.

149
00:11:03,096 --> 00:11:07,940
[SPEAKER_01]: What is this feeling that I'm feeling, that I'm designating with my verbal utterance, with my thoughts?

150
00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:10,682
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I referring to, basically, right here, right now?

151
00:11:14,959 --> 00:11:25,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, but all these concepts that I'm providing now as an answer, they are secondary to the very direction that I designated by saying feeling-feeling.

152
00:11:25,907 --> 00:11:26,888
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I feeling now?

153
00:11:26,948 --> 00:11:29,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it already implies that something is being felt.

154
00:11:29,670 --> 00:11:32,993
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now I'm asking what is being felt, while it is being felt.

155
00:11:33,893 --> 00:11:36,575
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it forces you to go against the grain, up the stream.

156
00:11:37,136 --> 00:11:41,039
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's so unpleasant, so ambiguous, so opaque, so dense, so obtuse.

157
00:11:42,723 --> 00:11:45,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the attention, everything goes down with the grain.

158
00:11:46,105 --> 00:11:51,108
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the yoniso manasikāra is the, like, the counterpart of ayoniso.

159
00:11:51,788 --> 00:11:56,551
[SPEAKER_01]: With the grain, yoniso is basically back to the womb of that grain.

160
00:12:01,714 --> 00:12:04,076
[SPEAKER_01]: And all of those types of assumptions are basically views.

161
00:12:08,599 --> 00:12:09,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally,

162
00:12:11,001 --> 00:12:15,804
[SPEAKER_01]: You think you have a view that you are, that you are your own, that you are your own master self-controller.

163
00:12:16,204 --> 00:12:17,725
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you think things belong to you.

164
00:12:20,607 --> 00:12:27,351
[SPEAKER_01]: And as you see, view cannot add anything to the aggregates, nor can take anything away from the actual aggregates.

165
00:12:29,352 --> 00:12:33,294
[SPEAKER_01]: It can only confuse the order and put first that which is second.

166
00:12:34,615 --> 00:12:37,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this sense, your sense of self, I am,

167
00:12:38,852 --> 00:12:46,434
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just so gratuitously assumed that it's seen as an independent entity that owns things, that chooses yes and says no.

168
00:12:48,695 --> 00:13:03,639
[SPEAKER_01]: But, once you realize that that's a wrong order, that it's not that you own things because you are, it's you are because you keep maintaining the ownership of things.

169
00:13:03,659 --> 00:13:06,720
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I said the possessions determine you.

170
00:13:07,270 --> 00:13:10,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Like that king would not have been known as a king.

171
00:13:10,572 --> 00:13:13,754
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't have been a king without having a possessions of a king.

172
00:13:15,154 --> 00:13:16,275
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not a king.

173
00:13:16,295 --> 00:13:16,935
[SPEAKER_01]: He defines you.

174
00:13:20,137 --> 00:13:22,539
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your body, your state, all of these are possessions.

175
00:13:24,980 --> 00:13:25,880
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you are.

176
00:13:26,141 --> 00:13:27,241
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you have sense of self.

177
00:13:28,022 --> 00:13:34,085
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't deny the sense of self, but you uproot it by seeing that it's not the owner, it's the result of ownership.

178
00:13:35,342 --> 00:13:43,070
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will uproot it when you see that that gratuitously assumed ownership of these things cannot be owned, because they have arisen on their own.

179
00:13:44,191 --> 00:13:53,319
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's only because they persist on their own, you can maintain your gratuitous taking at the face value as, oh, this presented itself for me.

180
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:54,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I must be.

181
00:13:54,881 --> 00:13:55,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I am.

182
00:13:55,962 --> 00:13:56,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, this is mine.

183
00:14:00,271 --> 00:14:03,872
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not analyzing things or breaking them into parts to deny their reality.

184
00:14:03,992 --> 00:14:04,132
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

185
00:14:04,472 --> 00:14:05,372
[SPEAKER_01]: You accept the reality.

186
00:14:06,693 --> 00:14:08,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the only way you can remove the ownership of it.

187
00:14:09,994 --> 00:14:12,834
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to break things into parts and say, you see, they're not really real.

188
00:14:13,414 --> 00:14:15,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Because atoms and electrons are constantly changing.

189
00:14:16,835 --> 00:14:21,617
[SPEAKER_01]: That's you now obscuring your ownership through denying the existence of things.

190
00:14:21,697 --> 00:14:22,377
[SPEAKER_01]: A is A.

191
00:14:22,977 --> 00:14:23,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is feeling.

192
00:14:23,797 --> 00:14:24,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception is perception.

193
00:14:24,918 --> 00:14:25,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Things are things.

194
00:14:25,678 --> 00:14:26,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Phenomena are phenomena.

195
00:14:27,463 --> 00:14:30,144
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha never said, oh, in the scene, there is no scene.

196
00:14:31,624 --> 00:14:31,784
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

197
00:14:32,745 --> 00:14:34,465
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, in the scene, there is just a scene.

198
00:14:34,665 --> 00:14:35,886
[SPEAKER_01]: In the arisen, there is just the arisen.

199
00:14:35,946 --> 00:14:36,786
[SPEAKER_01]: There was never a problem.

200
00:14:36,806 --> 00:14:39,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you then go and analyze that which has arisen?

201
00:14:39,567 --> 00:14:40,387
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it made of?

202
00:14:41,328 --> 00:14:44,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that it doesn't matter the amount of parts you discern.

203
00:14:45,849 --> 00:14:50,371
[SPEAKER_01]: The functional arrangement of that phenomena that gives the context to the parts is still very real.

204
00:14:51,111 --> 00:14:57,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what enables you to look at different parts without losing the sight of the thing that is being comprised by those parts.

205
00:15:02,816 --> 00:15:04,877
[SPEAKER_02]: You can see the dependence.

206
00:15:05,957 --> 00:15:06,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

207
00:15:06,937 --> 00:15:10,058
[SPEAKER_01]: The parts... Parts are within the context of the thing, which is what the thing is.

208
00:15:10,398 --> 00:15:19,180
[SPEAKER_01]: You denying the existence of the thing by saying it's made out of parts, it's basically failing to see that each part, then, is an individual thing as well.

209
00:15:19,961 --> 00:15:22,641
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you're just denying the existence of A because there is B.

210
00:15:23,562 --> 00:15:28,183
[SPEAKER_01]: See, feelings are not real because there are perceptions.

211
00:15:28,203 --> 00:15:32,064
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a complete, a magical assumption that these things somehow relate.

212
00:15:32,995 --> 00:15:34,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Or that they cancel each other out.

213
00:15:35,056 --> 00:15:42,661
[SPEAKER_01]: So, feeling is not real, because it's just hormones and chemicals and sensations running through your body.

214
00:15:42,721 --> 00:15:49,185
[SPEAKER_01]: So, would you know hormones and sensations as relatable to feeling, if that feeling is still not persisting right there?

215
00:15:49,405 --> 00:15:50,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's very real.

216
00:15:50,686 --> 00:15:53,308
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just on a different level of that generality where you're looking.

217
00:15:55,409 --> 00:15:59,492
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by focusing on the path, you are the only one that loses the sight.

218
00:16:01,061 --> 00:16:03,721
[SPEAKER_01]: of the general context, general image, the phenomena.

219
00:16:05,962 --> 00:16:10,483
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean that the phenomena disappears because you don't see it anymore.

220
00:16:10,703 --> 00:16:13,263
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still there, which is why you can keep looking at the parts.

221
00:16:14,803 --> 00:16:16,784
[SPEAKER_01]: And not like ask yourself, wait a minute, what is this now?

222
00:16:17,124 --> 00:16:21,845
[SPEAKER_01]: You know exactly what part you're looking of, which means the context is still at the peripheral of your mind.

223
00:16:22,345 --> 00:16:29,506
[SPEAKER_01]: But you've been negating to look in the peripheral and only focusing on what's in front of you actually, focusing on that, making a point of just staring at that,

224
00:16:30,279 --> 00:16:35,022
[SPEAKER_01]: That for you, you assume that this peripheral that is there, doesn't exist anymore.

225
00:16:36,043 --> 00:16:37,104
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I meant in the other talk.

226
00:16:37,144 --> 00:16:41,207
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, for most people, even their own thoughts feel like they're not real.

227
00:16:41,607 --> 00:16:47,411
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not concrete enough, because they got used to only the concrete palpable aspects of their experience to be the reality.

228
00:16:49,213 --> 00:16:51,594
[SPEAKER_01]: But if the thoughts were not real, how can you think them?

229
00:16:51,634 --> 00:16:52,455
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you talk about them?

230
00:16:53,896 --> 00:16:55,157
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you remember things you thought?

231
00:16:58,744 --> 00:16:59,084
[SPEAKER_01]: It's real.

232
00:16:59,625 --> 00:17:06,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever... That's what Buddha didn't say, whatever only material things are real or real or only mental things.

233
00:17:06,130 --> 00:17:10,573
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, whatever has the nature of manifestation, that's why it's real.

234
00:17:12,054 --> 00:17:15,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly where Sotāpati begins, understanding that.

235
00:17:16,078 --> 00:17:17,239
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where impermanence is.

236
00:17:18,039 --> 00:17:25,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, whatever has the nature of manifestation, whatever can manifest, whatever can appear, it cannot be mine.

237
00:17:28,478 --> 00:17:28,958
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it means.

238
00:17:28,978 --> 00:17:30,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever has the nature... Use the translation.

239
00:17:30,880 --> 00:17:33,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever has the nature of arising, has the nature of ceasing.

240
00:17:36,185 --> 00:17:40,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever is of nature to manifest, that's why it's impermanent.

241
00:17:41,009 --> 00:17:44,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it has a nature of manifestation on its own.

242
00:17:45,693 --> 00:17:51,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Not a nature of manifestation being created by me or coming from me.

243
00:17:51,719 --> 00:17:54,161
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you will force yourself

244
00:17:55,127 --> 00:17:59,968
[SPEAKER_01]: To uproot the gratuitous ownership of various manifestations.

245
00:17:59,988 --> 00:18:05,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Not through denying them, not through trying to destroy them, but by seeing that you can't own them, even if you want to.

246
00:18:06,350 --> 00:18:11,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you start uprooting the ownership, no sense of self can remain then maintained, fueled by that ownership.

247
00:18:12,672 --> 00:18:18,834
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, any view that you had that it's me, therefore things are mine, will fade and it will become inconceivable.

248
00:18:21,705 --> 00:18:25,549
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't matter whether you wish to be free from sense of self and the view of self.

249
00:18:26,529 --> 00:18:31,634
[SPEAKER_01]: If you stop maintaining the sense of ownership through right reflection, you will be free from the sense of self.

250
00:18:32,615 --> 00:18:48,029
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha meant when he said, like, even if a chicken... even if a hen sits on her eggs... No, if it sits on the eggs, but even if she doesn't wish, may my chicks pierce the shell and come out safely, they will, because she sits on them.

251
00:18:49,662 --> 00:18:57,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Or if a hen, even if she wishes that her chicks pierce out safely, but she doesn't sit on her eggs, they won't.

252
00:18:59,089 --> 00:19:07,976
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you wish, may I be free from sense of self and become an arahant, if you're not doing it rightly on the level of removing the ownership, you won't be free from those things.

253
00:19:08,817 --> 00:19:18,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't wish to be free from sense of self and wish to become an arahant, you will, if you start removing the ownership on account of understanding the independence of manifestation.

254
00:19:21,678 --> 00:19:23,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it will then remove the assumption.

255
00:19:24,359 --> 00:19:27,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumption is maintained through ignoring the basis of assumption.

256
00:19:28,319 --> 00:19:34,581
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the assumption of sense of self, sense of I am, is maintained through ignoring the fact that you are not the owner of things.

257
00:19:35,881 --> 00:19:36,701
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you act...

258
00:19:37,021 --> 00:19:40,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how... then, assumption becomes kind of its own fuel.

259
00:19:41,662 --> 00:19:46,663
[SPEAKER_01]: It fuels you ignoring, but it's the very presence of ignoring that fuels back the assumption.

260
00:19:46,703 --> 00:19:48,363
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a vicious circle, through and through.

261
00:19:49,975 --> 00:19:55,637
[SPEAKER_01]: That way, you can't break away from it accidentally or spontaneously or in a split of a second.

262
00:19:56,878 --> 00:20:00,400
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only break away by understanding it and then forcing yourself to go the other way.

263
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:05,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, everything is for me, but I'm told I should not regard it as mine.

264
00:20:06,420 --> 00:20:08,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have faith in what you were told, and you keep doing it.

265
00:20:09,463 --> 00:20:14,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it corresponds that things are actually not yours, that's how you get to see, because you stop fueling the assumption.

266
00:20:15,267 --> 00:20:21,052
[SPEAKER_01]: And after a while, when that views of those assumptions have faded, then you get to recognize what has faded.

267
00:20:21,993 --> 00:20:24,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Like what the Buddha said, like a carpenter using the axe.

268
00:20:24,275 --> 00:20:29,379
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't think, oh, today I'm wearing my handle thin this much, tomorrow I will do it this much.

269
00:20:29,419 --> 00:20:29,499
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

270
00:20:29,519 --> 00:20:35,724
[SPEAKER_01]: He just keeps doing it, and doing it, and doing it, until one day he looks at the handles, oh, I must replace it, because it's completely gone.

271
00:20:37,461 --> 00:20:38,704
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how assumptions fall off.

272
00:20:39,246 --> 00:20:44,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, I'm grasping, I refuse to grasp, thus I'm free from this upādāna.

273
00:20:44,961 --> 00:20:46,305
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only wish that it's that easy.

